From ab5dd01cd696b806c761bc4e985207db57887e0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 23:51:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 173/209] fix: structured sensor noise was orders of magnitude
 too loud, and the integration exponent goes to 0

Two things, both on the digital families only. Film is untouched.

A. The structured terms were tuned blind while the multiplicative path clamped them.

The additive linear path of e273c308 exposed their true magnitude and the user
photographed the result: CCD at ISO 777 in daylight showed full-frame vertical
COLOUR streaks of tens of percent, Phone showed strong horizontal bands.

Three separate faults, all in the column/smear term and the way the structured
terms were drawn:

 1. smearE used the BARE full well - smear * 0.01 * fullWell - so it kept its
    absolute electron count while the signal shrank with the ISO gain. Against
    the picture it therefore grew by iso/isoBase: x4 at ISO 800 on a CCD whose
    base is 200, x64 at ISO 12800. It is now a fraction of the SATURATING
    COLUMN'S CHARGE AT THIS ISO, fullWell * isoBase/iso, which is what a
    charge-transfer artefact is and is ISO-invariant relative to the signal.
    CCD, S35, green, sigma of the column term in code values of 255:
        before   ISO 200  1.04 CV       ISO 12800   66.4 CV   (at mid grey!)
        after    ISO 200  0.00 CV       ISO 12800    0.00 CV  (at mid grey)
                          0.46 CV at x = 0.9, and the same 0.46 CV at ISO 12800
 2. the gate had a FLOOR of 0.3, so a third of the column term was on every
    pixel of every frame - a full-frame vertical texture, not a streak. The
    gate is now smoothstep(0.75, 1.0, luma): nothing at all in a normally
    exposed scene, a faint streak only under a clipping source. Red at x = 0.9
    is 0.88 CV, inside the ~2 CV ceiling the physics allows for a term this
    crude.
 3. row and column were drawn as THREE INDEPENDENT Gaussians, so a luma band
    came out as a rainbow band. One amplifier serves the whole line or column,
    so the offset it adds is a single number the three channels share; both
    terms are now sqrt(0.9) * one shared draw + sqrt(0.1) * a per-channel draw
    (unit variance per channel, 10 % of the energy as colour).

Presets back to physical electron numbers, at the quiet end of the measured
ranges because every real body corrects these with optical-black reference
rows and columns: CMOS row 0.3 -> 0.2 e-, CCD DSNU 4.0 -> 3.0 e- and row
1.5 -> 1.2 e-, Phone DSNU 0.5 -> 1.0 e- and row 0.5 -> 0.3 e-. PRNU unchanged
(0.5 / 0.8 / 1.0 %, which is the measured range). Phone row noise at -4 stops,
ISO base: 0.031 CV green / 0.059 CV red, against the 1 CV ceiling.

r_FilmGrainDebug 3 now prints ONE LINE PER TERM - shot, read, PRNU, DSNU, row,
column - in sigma code values at mid grey and at -4 stops, plus the column term
at x = 0.9 where it lives, and the quadrature total. The magnitudes can never
be blind again, which is the actual fix.

B. The integration exponent defaults to 0 (the 1:1 view).

The user judges the CMOS family too clean against real cameras; visual feedback
over maths. All three presets carry E = 0 (was 0.7). The shader's compile-time
FILMGRAIN_INTEGRATION_EXP stays 0.7 for reference and the knob stays on the bus
(Grain_User_Digital3.x). Phone frame stacking is a separate divisor, unchanged.

With E = 0 the downscale ratio has NO effect on noise, so the format had to
carry its own or a M43 body would be identical to a full frame. It does now,
physically: the preset fixes a photosite COUNT and a REFERENCE BODY WIDTH and
derives the pitch as bodyWidth / count, and full-well capacity follows photosite
AREA. A smaller body divides the same count into a smaller width, gets a smaller
photosite, collects fewer electrons and is noisier. A body wider than the
reference gets no bonus - a family's electronics are designed for a format.
CMOS 6000 across 36 mm (6.0 um), CCD 4300 across 36 mm (8.4 um), Phone 8000
across 7.6 mm (0.95 um).

CMOS, sigma in code values at -4 stops, ISO 800, Grain Strength 1, green:
    full frame   6.00 um   60 000 e-   0.68 CV
    APS-C        3.93 um   25 785 e-   1.04 CV
    M43          2.88 um   13 856 e-   1.43 CV     = 2.08x the full frame
which is almost exactly the two stops of real-world "equivalence".

Grain_User_Digital3.y now carries the effective photosite pitch so the report
cannot print a pitch and a full well that disagree.

Built Profile with the bundled CMake 3.22, sequential, no clean: CryRenderD3D11
and CinematicCamera, 0 errors / 0 warnings. FilmGrain.cfi also compiled
standalone with fxc -T ps_5_0 against a stub PS_ScreenSize, clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
